// Title: Tasks Folder Evasion
// Sigma ID: cc4e02ba-9c06-48e2-b09e-2500cace9ae0
// Status: test | Level: high
// Author: Sreeman
// Date: 2020-01-13 | Modified: 2022-12-25
// ATT&CK: privilege-escalation, persistence, execution, stealth, T1574.001
// Description: The Tasks folder in system32 and syswow64 are globally writable paths. Adversaries can take
//   advantage of this and load or influence any script hosts or ANY .NET Application in Tasks to
//   load and execute a custom assembly into cscript, wscript, regsvr32, mshta, eventvwr
// False positives:
//   - Unknown
// References:
//   - https://web.archive.org/web/20200116124158/https://twitter.com/subTee/status/1216465628946563073
//   - https://gist.github.com/am0nsec/8378da08f848424e4ab0cc5b317fdd26
// Sigma rule: https://github.com/SigmaHQ/sigma/blob/d7c9da10c170a6988d68ebdf2c9f7bd8782b7c55/rules/windows/process_creation/proc_creation_win_susp_task_folder_evasion.yml
// License: Detection Rule License (DRL) 1.1 - https://github.com/SigmaHQ/Detection-Rule-License
// Converted by sigma2kql (target: microsoft_xdr, pySigma 1.5.1, pySigma-backend-kusto 1.0.1)
// Validation: passed (Kusto.Language 12.4.1, live Defender schema)
// Machine-generated and not tested against real telemetry. Verify before use as a detection.

DeviceProcessEvents
| where (ProcessCommandLine contains "echo " or ProcessCommandLine contains "copy " or ProcessCommandLine contains "type " or ProcessCommandLine contains "file createnew") and (ProcessCommandLine contains " C:\\Windows\\System32\\Tasks\\" or ProcessCommandLine contains " C:\\Windows\\SysWow64\\Tasks\\")
